course: ai
lesson: 12
title: Keep yourself private, and voices that lie
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Your full name, home address, school and photos in your uniform are like the keys to your front door: you don't hand them to strangers, and an app is a stranger. What you type or upload may be stored by a company you have never met.
explain: AI can also copy things that used to be hard to fake. It was reported that scammers can copy a person's voice from a short clip online, so Chloe's aunt got a call that sounded exactly like her crying nephew asking for money.
explain: The fix is old-fashioned and strong: hang up, call the person back on their usual number, and agree a silly family password that is written nowhere online.
explain: Pictures and videos can be faked too: a shark swimming down a flooded street, a famous YouTuber "giving away" phones, a hand with six fingers. Sometimes there are clues, but often you can't tell by looking, so ask who posted it first.
explain: And bots are not doctors or friends: a sore tummy or a sad feeling goes to a grown-up who knows you.
fact: Never share your full name, address or school with a bot or an app.
fact: Ask a grown-up before uploading any photo, and never upload ones showing your school badge or home.
fact: Good bots point you to real help; sad feelings that last should be told to a trusted adult.
fact: Bots can list frightening and wrong illnesses; when you feel ill, tell a grown-up, who can call a doctor.
fact: It was reported that scammers use AI to copy voices from short clips found online.
fact: If a call sounds like family asking for money, hang up and call them back on their usual number.
fact: A family password, a silly word that is not online anywhere, beats a fake voice.
fact: A deepfake is a fake video or voice made with AI to look or sound like a real person.
fact: Giveaways asking for card details are scams, even if a famous face seems to promise them.
fact: Many viral pictures are fake or edited, so check who posted them first before sharing.
fact: AI pictures sometimes have clues, like odd hands or nonsense writing, but newer ones may have none.
fact: Making or sharing fake pictures of real people to mock them is bullying and can be against the law.
exercise: A bot asks Mia for her name and address "to help better". What should she do? | Not share them, and tell a grown-up | Share them, because bots keep everything secret | Share only her address, not her name | Give a friend's address instead
exercise: Sam uploads a photo in his school uniform to a cartoon app. What is the problem? | His face and school badge are now with a company | The cartoon will look nothing like him, so it's a waste of time | Uniform photos are illegal to take | The app will send the photo to his teacher
exercise: Lily tells a bot she feels sad all the time. It suggests a trusted adult. Who could that be? | A parent, a teacher or another adult she trusts | Another chatbot that is even kinder than this one | A stranger in a game chat | Nobody, she should keep it to herself
exercise: Connor's tummy hurts and the bot lists scary illnesses. What should he do? | Tell a grown-up, who can call a doctor if needed | Pick the illness on the list that sounds most likely | Ask three more bots for a second opinion | Take whatever medicine is in the cupboard
exercise: Chloe's aunt gets a call in her nephew's voice asking for money. What is the safest move? | Hang up and call him back on his usual number | Send the money quickly, because he's crying and scared | Ask the caller for his bank details | Keep him talking until the money is sent
exercise: Maddie's family picks a silly family password. What makes it a good one? | It is known only to them and isn't online anywhere | It is her pet's name, the one from her profile page | It is "password" so nobody forgets | It is posted on the fridge by the window
exercise: A video shows a famous YouTuber giving away free phones if you enter your parents' card details. What is it? | A scam, perhaps with a deepfake video | A real giveaway for lucky fans who are quick | A game where nobody loses money | A test from the bank
exercise: Everyone is sharing a photo of a shark in a flooded street. What should Mia ask first? | Who posted it first, and is it on trusted news? | How big is the shark? | Which friend shared it first? | Why didn't the shark eat anyone?
exercise: Sam sees a waving girl in an AI picture with six fingers. What is that? | A clue the picture was made by AI | Proof the girl is from another planet | A normal hand drawn quickly | A clue the camera was broken
exercise: Lily's brother says the cat piano video is AI. Can she always tell by looking? | No, often you can't tell just by looking | Yes, AI cats always have six legs, so just count | Yes, real cats can't sit at pianos | No, because all cat videos are AI
exercise: A classmate makes a fake silly picture of a boy and shares it in the class group. What is it? | Bullying, and it can be against the law | A harmless joke if it's funny | Fine, because it's not a real photo of him | Only wrong if a teacher sees it
exercise: What is a deepfake? | A fake video or voice made with AI | A very deep swimming pool | A secret password | A kind of computer virus
exercise: Why can't you trust a phone voice alone any more? | AI can copy voices from short clips | Phones change every voice a little bit on purpose | Voices sound different on Tuesdays | Scammers always use their own voice
exercise: Do AI pictures always have clues like extra fingers? | No, newer ones may have none | Yes, every AI picture has six fingers on each hand | Yes, AI writing is always perfect | No, AI cannot draw hands at all
test: "it's only a cartoon app, who cares if it sees my face" Why care? | The company keeps your face and maybe your school badge | Cartoon apps make your face disappear from all your photos | Only real photos are risky, cartoons aren't | The app will make you look silly forever
test: What would happen if Gran's "grandson" on the phone couldn't say the family password? | She would know it might be a fake and hang up | She should send double the money just to be safe | She should give him a new password | Nothing, voices never lie
test: "bot said my headache's prob a brain thing, i'm freaking out" Best reply? | Bots list scary guesses; tell a grown-up | Believe it and go to bed | Ask the bot for the cure | Search for more scary illnesses
test: In a cartoon, a shark photo goes viral and everyone believes it at once. What should the characters have done? | Checked who posted it first and trusted news | Shared it faster than everyone else in town | Gone outside to look for the shark | Added a bigger shark and reshared it
test: Your friend says the free phone giveaway must be real "because it's his actual face and voice". Why not? | Faces and voices can be faked with AI | Famous people always give away phones to fans | Fakes can't use real faces | Only bad phones are given away
test: "nah it's just a laugh, it's not even a real photo of him" about a fake picture of a classmate. What's true? | It can hurt him and is bullying | It's fine because it's fake | It's fine if he laughs too | It's only bullying if it's real
test: Which family password is best? | "purple-teapot-wobble", made up and never posted | Your dog's name from your profile | Your birthday | "1234"
test: A video of a cat playing piano perfectly, just for fun. Does it matter much if it's AI? | Less for fun videos; it matters a lot for news | Yes, sharing fun AI videos is a crime | It matters more than any news story | No, nothing online ever matters
test: Why should you never type your school's name into a chatbot? | What you type may be stored and seen by others | Bots don't know any schools, so it's no use | It makes the bot slower | Schools have copyright on their names, so it's against the law
test: Lily's bot was kind when she felt sad. What should she still do? | Tell a trusted adult | Only talk to the bot from now on | Wait until the feeling goes alone | Ask the bot to tell her parents
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a deepfake? | A video or recording fabricated by artificial intelligence to resemble a real person | A recording of genuine events made at great depth | A password of exceptional length | A computer virus transmitted by electronic mail
exam: It is given that a telephone call, in the voice of a relative, requests the urgent transfer of money. Identify the most prudent course of action. | Terminating the call and contacting the relative by the customary number | Transferring the money without delay | Furnishing the caller with bank details | Prolonging the call until the money is dispatched
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning personal information and chatbots. | Name, address and school ought not to be disclosed, such information being liable to storage | Personal information is destroyed upon closure of the application | Chatbots are prohibited by law from retaining any information | Disclosure is safe provided the request is courteously worded
exam: Ascertain the status of the fabrication and circulation of a mocking image of a classmate. | It constitutes bullying and may be contrary to law | It is permissible provided the image is fictitious | It is permissible provided the classmate is amused | It is a matter of no consequence
